9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in 
`MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`, stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and 
opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with 
server-fenced attempt generations and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; 
application-level recovery and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, 
members-only typing indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including 
rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open 
conversation after reload.
15:## 3. Goals and success metrics
27:### Success metrics
28:- With ≤ 10 pending messages and create quota available, all pending messages are delivered within 10 s after 
connectivity returns, without any click, in their original order, each exactly once; with more, delivery continues 
automatically within the server rate limit (D1).
51:- Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies, written before 
dispatch; item states; cancel; per-conversation ordered automatic drain across tabs; claim leases and recovery; 
server-fenced attempts; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-end and sign-out 
rules; sending blocked with a clear message when durable storage is unavailable (D5).
59:- Messaging metrics as redacted structured server logs via an authenticated, rate-limited, size-bounded application 
route.
69:- Usage/engagement metrics; a metrics UI or external analytics service.
77:- J1 Offline send: A loses internet and keeps writing; each message appears as pending; the banner says "Sem 
conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; with ≤ 10 pending and quota available, every 
pending message is delivered within 10 s in order and B sees each once; with more, delivery continues within the rate 
limit (D1).
94:| FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or 
periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, always with their own `clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and 
quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of connectivity returning; with more, delivery continues within the 
rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent message and leaves the outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
100:| FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for a winning insertion (including among concurrent 
requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every 
create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and 
fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
101:| FR-009 | An item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send is never sent automatically: once fenced as not 
stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; an unknown item found stored resolves as 
delivered, never as expired. "Tentar de novo" on an expired item keeps the key and the original send time (for 
metrics) and starts a new 24 h delivery window, within which the normal transient/unknown rules apply. | Must | 
AC-010, AC-042 |
119:| FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send 
outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage duration, polling activations and duration — in a 
bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at 
most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user 
rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, 
drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
120:| FR-028 | Metrics never block, slow, or break messaging: messaging code never awaits metric delivery; an 
erroring, hanging, or overloaded metrics route changes no messaging behavior or timing and shows no error. | Must | 
AC-029 |
144:| BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only closed-enum event type and outcome/reason, bounded durations/counts, 
conversation type, timestamp, and server-derived app version — never message text, attachment names, conversation 
names, user names or emails, user/conversation identifiers, or any client-supplied free text. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, 
confirmation point 9; review r2 #14 |
145:| BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the phase (written + applied locally with recorded 
rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only with explicit owner authorization, 
followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is deployed by the owner after the migrations; 
rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
158:- Database: attempt-generation state per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced or stale generation 
cannot commit; the original submitted composition used for replay comparison; mention records written atomically with 
the message; private typing-topic authorization (RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = auth.uid()::text` → 
`users.id` membership); `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` without `anon` 
EXECUTE; rate-limit buckets for all create requests and for fence/lookup calls, separate from the insertion quota.
159:- API: create route sender and attempt-generation fencing, winning-insert quota charging, request budget, 
`clientMessageId` in responses; fence/lookup route for the caller's own message only; authenticated, rate-limited, 
size-bounded metrics route.
160:- Structured metric log lines (BR-013).
166:- Hosting/Supabase logs as the metrics sink; no new external analytics dependency.
175:- No message text, names, or identifiers in metrics (BR-013); no external analytics service.
179:- Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: 
claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events / 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s metrics 
abort.
200:| Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests | Items stay pending, retried automatically; quota 
charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate budget (AC-009). |
220:| Malicious, oversized, hanging, or sustained metrics traffic | Rejected or dropped; route rate-limited; messaging 
unaffected (AC-028, AC-029). |
230:- Metrics: closed enums, bounded numbers and sizes, server-derived version, per-user rate limit, no free text 
(BR-013); unauthenticated requests are rejected and produce no metric log line.
271:- AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route 
writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, and server-derived version; a scan finds no message 
text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, 
or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; a request over 50 events or 16 KB is rejected; requests 
beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an unauthenticated request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
272:- AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and separately hangs, and separately is flooded with sustained 
traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most one metrics 
request is in flight from A's tab, a hanging metrics request is aborted after 5 s, the client buffer never exceeds its 
bound (oldest events dropped), and no error is shown.
277:- AC-034 (BR-014, §10): Every migration of the phase is applied locally with recorded rollback SQL; before any 
online application, the currently hosted build (git ref confirmed by the owner) and the Phase 4 build each pass a 
send/read/unread smoke against the migrated local database (legacy unkeyed writes still work); at phase end, after 
explicit owner authorization, it is applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`, the catalog is read back from that target, and 
a runtime smoke check passes; the hand-off states the app is ready to deploy after the migrations.
278:- AC-035 (§12): The Supabase/RLS review gate passes for every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API 
route change.
298:  - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): sender and attempt-generation fencing 
incl. stale requests and stale fences (AC-044, AC-045), insertion quota, request budget, and fence budget (AC-009), 
fence/lookup authorization incl. anon and other users' keys (AC-038, AC-042, AC-044), outcome classes (AC-046), 
mention atomicity/validation/replay/RLS (AC-024, AC-046), private typing-topic authorization incl. 
anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), `get_unread_counts` anon revoke 
(AC-031), metrics route auth, enums, sizes, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
305:- Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream 
Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and stale polls, typing tab close and conversation 
switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification 
race, delayed notification authorization, malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost 
access.
310:- Supabase/RLS review gate passed (AC-035).
318:- Non-blocking: per-company or per-conversation metric breakdowns would need identifiers in logs, which BR-013 
forbids; a later owner decision is required to add them.
